/-!
# Kähler differentials

Let `R → A → B` be commutative rings with `A → B` surjective and `I = ker(A → B)`. The conormal
(or naive cotangent) sequence
`I/I² → B ⊗[A] Ω[A⁄R] → Ω[B⁄R] → 0`
is exact; its left hand map is Mathlib's `KaehlerDifferential.kerCotangentToTensor`, recorded here
`B`-linearly.

If moreover the square-zero surjection `P ⧸ I² → S` admits a section as a map of `R`-algebras,
where now `I = ker(P → S)`, then the conormal sequence of `P → S` is a *split* short exact sequence
`0 → I/I² → S ⊗[P] Ω[P⁄R] → Ω[S⁄R] → 0`: it is isomorphic to the trivial one, by an isomorphism
`S ⊗[P] Ω[P⁄R] ≃ I/I² × Ω[S⁄R]` carrying the left hand map to the inclusion of the first factor
and the right hand map to the projection onto the second. The mechanism is a retraction of the
left hand map, induced by the `R`-derivation `P ⧸ I² → I/I²`, `x ↦ x - g(x mod I)`.

That `Ω[R[x_1, …, x_n]⁄R]` is free on the differentials of the variables, that
`Ω[C⁄A] → Ω[C⁄B]` continues the sequence of a tower `A → B → C`, that `Ω[S⁄R]` is a finitely
presented `S`-module as soon as `S` is a finitely presented `R`-algebra, and that it vanishes
when `R → S` is surjective, are all Mathlib's.

## Main definitions

* `ConormalSeq.cotangentModule`: the `B`-module structure on `I/I²` transported along `A ⧸ I ≃ B`.
* `ConormalSeq.conormalMap`: the conormal map `I/I² →ₗ[B] B ⊗[A] Ω[A⁄R]`.

## Main results

* `ConormalSeq.conormal_exact_sequence`: the conormal sequence of a surjection is exact.
* `KaehlerDifferential.split_exact_kerCotangentToTensor_mapBaseChange_of_section`: the conormal
  sequence splits as a product as soon as `P ⧸ I² → S` has a section as a map of `R`-algebras.
* `KaehlerDifferential.map_comp_map`: the map on relative differentials induced by a composite
  is the composite of the induced maps.
* `KaehlerDifferential.map_base_comp_map`: the same for a composite of bases — enlarging the base
  after the fact gives the map induced by the enlarged base.
* `KaehlerDifferential.span_range_D_of_base`: differentials generated by the differentials of a
  family of elements stay generated by them after the base is enlarged.
* `KaehlerDifferential.span_union_range_map_D_of_adjoin_singleton_eq_top`: for `B` generated as
  an `A`-algebra by one element `x`, `Ω[B⁄Λ]` is generated over `B` by the differentials pulled
  back from `A` together with `d x`.

## Implementation notes

`KaehlerDifferential.kerCotangentToTensor` is `A`-linear, and `Ideal.Cotangent I` carries the
`A ⧸ I`-module structure rather than a `B`-module one; the latter exists precisely because `A → B`
is surjective, so the conormal sequence is restated `B`-linearly along `A ⧸ I ≃ B`
(`ConormalSeq.cotangentModule`).

Split exactness is recorded in the strongest of its equivalent forms, the decomposition of the
middle term carrying the two maps of the sequence to `LinearMap.inl` and `LinearMap.snd`; the
one-sided forms — injectivity of the left hand map, a retraction of it, a section of the right
hand map — follow from it in one step, as they do from one another by `Function.Exact.split_tfae`.
The retraction that produces the decomposition is `retractionKerCotangentToTensorEquivSection`,
which packages the derivation `x ↦ x - g(x mod I)` (`derivationOfSectionOfKerSqZero`) with the
isomorphism `S ⊗[P ⧸ I²] Ω[(P ⧸ I²)⁄R] ≃ S ⊗[P] Ω[P⁄R]` of [StacksProject 02HQ].

The hypothesis of that statement is a section of `P ⧸ I² → S` rather than of `P → S`: composing a
section of `P → S` with `P → P ⧸ I²` gives one of `P ⧸ I² → S`, so this is the weaker hypothesis,
and it is the one the lifting property of a formally smooth algebra produces. Surjectivity of
`P → S` is then a consequence rather than a hypothesis, since `P → P ⧸ I²` is surjective and the
section splits `P ⧸ I² → S`. The `S`-module structure on `I/I²` is therefore unavailable there and
the conclusion is asserted `P`-linearly; that is no weaker, since restriction of scalars along a
surjection is an equivalence on maps of modules annihilated by its kernel
(`LinearMap.extendScalarsOfSurjectiveEquiv`), and both factors of the product are.

